  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nHeading into early next week, there is good confidence that a \nPacific low will move to the central/southern coast of California \nby the end of the day Monday. Exactly how this low evolves once\nreaching the coast is still a little uncertain, but latest model \nconsensus suggests the low will track ENE inland staying mostly \nnorth of the local area and driving a chance for showers and storms \nacross northern AZ. Conceptually this is not a bad setup for a \ntransitional severe weather event with potential frontogenetical \nforcing and a moderately strong upper level jet with higher shear, \nbut rebounding quickly from the very dry air this weekend and \ngetting sufficient moisture in here to support any lower desert rain \nchance will be tough. Model guidance does at least support a slow \nreturn of moisture Monday through next week, more so coming from the \nsoutheast like typical early stages of the monsoon. \n\nModel guidance is also pointing toward a strong ridge developing \nthrough the middle of the U.S. with the subtropical high \nrestrengthening near/over Texas by mid to late week. This would keep \nthe Desert Southwest under predominant southwest flow which is not \nthe most ideal setup for lower desert storm chances, especially not \nfor southeast CA and southwest AZ. Latest NBM forecast has PoPs \nfocused primarily across northern and eastern AZ each day next week \nwith only minor (10-20%) PoPs for south-central AZ lower deserts in \nthe evenings. The one thing this pattern does open the door for, the \nlonger it persists, is potential Eastern Pacific tropical moisture \nadvecting north.\n\nTemperatures through next week will mostly stay the same day-to-day, \nwith highs in the upper 90s to lower 100s, which will be around 2-5 \ndegrees above normal, and lows remaining mostly in the 70s. The \ntemperatures will lead to widespread Minor HeatRisk through at least \nthe first half of the week with a trend toward areas of Moderate \nHeatRisk by the second half of the week as morning low temperatures \ncreep back up.",
